#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/msgpackr/README.md:261
msgid "Custom Extensions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/msgpackr/README.md:262
msgid ""
"You can add your own custom extensions, which can be used to encode "
"specific types/classes in certain ways. This is done by using the "
"`addExtension` function, and specifying the class, extension `type` code "
"(should be a number from 1-100, reserving negatives for MessagePack, "
"101-127 for msgpackr), and your `pack` and `unpack` functions (or just "
"the one you need)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/msgpackr/README.md:263
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"import { addExtension, Packr } from 'msgpackr';\n"
"\n"
"class MyCustomClass {...}\n"
"\n"
"let extPackr = new Packr();\n"
"addExtension({\n"
"\tClass: MyCustomClass,\n"
"\ttype: 11, // register your own extension code (a type code from 1-100)\n"
"\tpack(instance) {\n"
"\t\t// define how your custom class should be encoded\n"
"\t\treturn Buffer.from([instance.myData]); // return a buffer\n"
"\t},\n"
"\tunpack(buffer) {\n"
"\t\t// define how your custom class should be decoded\n"
"\t\tlet instance = new MyCustomClass();\n"
"\t\tinstance.myData = buffer[0];\n"
"\t\treturn instance; // decoded value from buffer\n"
"\t}\n"
"});\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/msgpackr/README.md:284
msgid ""
"If you want to use msgpackr to encode and decode the data within your "
"extensions, you can use the `read` and `write` functions and read and "
"write data/objects that will be encoded and decoded by msgpackr, which "
"can be easier and faster than creating and receiving separate buffers:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/msgpackr/README.md:286
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"import { addExtension, Packr } from 'msgpackr';\n"
"\n"
"class MyCustomClass {...}\n"
"\n"
"let extPackr = new Packr();\n"
"addExtension({\n"
"\tClass: MyCustomClass,\n"
"\ttype: 11, // register your own extension code (a type code from 1-100)\n"
"\twrite(instance) {\n"
"\t\t// define how your custom class should be encoded\n"
"\t\treturn instance.myData; // return some data to be encoded\n"
"\t}\n"
"\tread(data) {\n"
"\t\t// define how your custom class should be decoded,\n"
"\t\t// data will already be unpacked/decoded\n"
"\t\tlet instance = new MyCustomClass();\n"
"\t\tinstance.myData = data;\n"
"\t\treturn instance; // return decoded value\n"
"\t}\n"
"});\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/msgpackr/README.md:308
msgid ""
"Note that you can just return the same object from `write`, and in this "
"case msgpackr will encode it using the default object/array encoding:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/msgpackr/README.md:309
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"addExtension({\n"
"\tClass: MyCustomClass,\n"
"\ttype: 12,\n"
"\tread: function(data) {\n"
"\t\tObject.setPrototypeOf(data, MyCustomClass.prototype)\n"
"\t\treturn data\n"
"\t},\n"
"\twrite: function(data) {\n"
"\t\treturn data\n"
"\t}\n"
"})\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/msgpackr/README.md:322
msgid ""
"You can also create an extension with `Class` and `write` methods, but no"
" `type` (or `read`), if you just want to customize how a class is "
"serialized without using MessagePack extension encoding."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/msgpackr/README.md:324
msgid "Additional Performance Optimizations"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/msgpackr/README.md:325
msgid "Msgpackr is already fast, but here are some tips for making it faster:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/msgpackr/README.md:327
msgid "Buffer Reuse"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/msgpackr/README.md:328
msgid ""
"Msgpackr is designed to work well with reusable buffers. Allocating new "
"buffers can be relatively expensive, so if you have Node addons, it can "
"be much faster to reuse buffers and use memcpy to copy data into existing"
" buffers. Then msgpackr `unpack` can be executed on the same buffer, with"
" new data, and optionally take a second paramter indicating the effective"
" size of the available data in the buffer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/msgpackr/README.md:330
msgid "Arena Allocation (`useBuffer()`)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/msgpackr/README.md:331
msgid ""
"During the serialization process, data is written to buffers. Again, "
"allocating new buffers is a relatively expensive process, and the "
"`useBuffer` method can help allow reuse of buffers that will further "
"improve performance. With `useBuffer` method, you can provide a buffer, "
"serialize data into it, and when it is known that you are done using that"
" buffer, you can call `useBuffer` again to reuse it. The use of "
"`useBuffer` is never required, buffers will still be handled and cleaned "
"up through GC if not used, it just provides a small performance boost."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/msgpackr/README.md:333
msgid "Record Structure Extension Definition"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/msgpackr/README.md:334
msgid ""
"The record struction extension uses extension id 0x72 (\"r\") to declare "
"the use of this functionality. The extension \"data\" byte (or bytes) "
"identifies the byte or bytes used to identify the start of a record in "
"the subsequent MessagePack block or stream. The identifier byte (or the "
"first byte in a sequence) must be from 0x40 - 0x7f (and therefore "
"replaces one byte representations of positive integers 64 - 127, which "
"can alternately be represented with int or uint types). The extension "
"declaration must be immediately follow by an MessagePack array that "
"defines the field names of the record structure."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/msgpackr/README.md:336
msgid ""
"Once a record identifier and record field names have been defined, the "
"parser/decoder should proceed to read the next value. Any subsequent use "
"of the record identifier as a value in the block or stream should parsed "
"as a record instance, and the next n values, where is n is the number of "
"fields (as defined in the array of field names), should be read as the "
"values of the fields. For example, here we have defined a structure with "
"fields \"foo\" and \"bar\", with the record identifier 0x40, and then "
"read a record instance that defines the field values of 4 and 2, "
"respectively:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/msgpackr/README.md:337
msgid ""
"+--------+--------+--------+~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~+--------+--------+\n"
"|  0xd4  |  0x72  |  0x40  | array: [ \"foo\", \"bar\" ] |  0x04  |  0x02"
"  |\n"
"+--------+--------+--------+~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~+--------+--------+\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/msgpackr/README.md:342
msgid "Which should generate an object that would correspond to JSON:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/msgpackr/README.md:343
#, python-brace-format
msgid "{ \"foo\": 4, \"bar\": 2}\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/msgpackr/README.md:347
msgid "Additional value types"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/msgpackr/README.md:348
msgid ""
"msgpackr supports `undefined` (using fixext1 + type: 0 + data: 0 to match"
" other JS implementations), `NaN`, `Infinity`, and `-Infinity` (using "
"standard IEEE 754 representations with doubles/floats)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/msgpackr/README.md:350
msgid "Dates"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/msgpackr/README.md:351
msgid ""
"msgpackr saves all JavaScript `Date`s using the standard MessagePack date"
" extension (type -1), using the smallest of 32-bit, 64-bit or 96-bit "
"format needed to store the date without data loss (or using 32-bit if "
"useTimestamp32 options is specified)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/msgpackr/README.md:354
msgid ""
"With structured cloning enabled, msgpackr will also use extensions to "
"store Set, Map, Error, RegExp, ArrayBufferView objects and preserve their"
" types."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/msgpackr/README.md:356
msgid "Alternate Encoding/Package"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/msgpackr/README.md:357
msgid ""
"The high-performance serialization and deserialization algorithms in the "
"msgpackr package are also available in the "
"[cbor-x](https://github.com/kriszyp/cbor-x) for the CBOR format, with the"
" same API and design. A quick summary of the pros and cons of using "
"MessagePack vs CBOR are:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/msgpackr/README.md:358
msgid ""
"MessagePack has wider adoption, and, at least with this implementation is"
" slightly more efficient (by roughly 1%)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/msgpackr/README.md:359
msgid ""
"CBOR has an [official IETF standardization "
"track](https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc7049), and the record extensions is"
" conceptually/philosophically a better fit for CBOR tags."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/msgpackr/README.md:361
msgid "License"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/msgpackr/README.md:363
msgid "MIT"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/msgpackr/README.md:365
msgid "Browser Consideration"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/msgpackr/README.md:366
msgid ""
"MessagePack can be a great choice for high-performance data delivery to "
"browsers, as reasonable data size is possible without compression. And "
"msgpackr works very well in modern browsers. However, it is worth noting "
"that if you want highly compact data, brotli or gzip are most effective "
"in compressing, and MessagePack's character frequency tends to defeat "
"Huffman encoding used by these standard compression algorithms, resulting"
" in less compact data than compressed JSON."
msgstr ""

